{:edb/db-before-t 2230 :edb/db-after-t 2231 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535543 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:10:04.549Z" 13194139535543 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354497 13194139535543 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354503 13194139535543 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354527 13194139535543 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354509 13194139535543 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354518 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354498 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1089 "q-339746" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1113 #uuid "eb5c3ca0-3507-57c3-ab49-eeb884b54da6" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354497 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.00.$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354499 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354500 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354501 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354502 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1041 "field-1" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354502 13194139535543 true] [17592186354498 1110 #uuid "58dcd14e-7b03-5264-8d1d-f06032a3fa02" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354499 1034 "1.21" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354499 1111 #uuid "6d7ab00d-cd37-515a-beb9-f62ff448e618" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354500 1034 "0.03" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354500 1111 #uuid "45199a01-42f8-5382-9e98-b7738bca85e2" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354501 1034 "2.31" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354501 1111 #uuid "78353621-d875-57df-8dd0-fbb367e2acde" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354502 1034 "1.00" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354502 1111 #uuid "de09f742-6633-5580-9dcc-5cd239ed6a45" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354504 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1089 "q-339755" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3}),$ rounded to two decimal places\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1113 #uuid "5bf7de1b-3e49-5cb2-9cc7-c1247a864f21" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354503 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{3}-0=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+2y+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈0.25.$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354505 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354506 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354507 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354508 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1041 "field-1" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354507 13194139535543 true] [17592186354504 1110 #uuid "e2a633da-d67c-5ca9-bfe5-7e30dd02a524" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354505 1034 "1.42" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354505 1111 #uuid "d1c04133-a9b8-5fbb-936f-e68c27ded630" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354506 1034 "0.43" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354506 1111 #uuid "c447796c-92fd-50a7-8b00-8e9920f7e84a" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354507 1034 "0.25" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354507 1111 #uuid "5a61fa4b-efcd-5c99-80f7-e0bc43b9cd89" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354508 1034 "1.25" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354508 1111 #uuid "0f7b4238-e778-5aa0-a054-d6a92be8ca59" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354510 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354514 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1089 "q-339865" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1113 #uuid "4a6c86e8-ee41-5d34-a9e5-2474fc7e8afa" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354509 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=3.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+6\\cdot (3.25)) \\\\  & =10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.25,\\,y(1)≈10.50.\n$$" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354511 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354512 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354513 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1041 "field-1" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354513 13194139535543 true] [17592186354510 1110 #uuid "c4e29467-09a8-5fba-9e0b-2488a2dfdf74" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354511 1034 "2.89" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354511 1111 #uuid "9fdaecd3-6323-5972-8194-aa71d7243ea0" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354512 1034 "-3.50" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354512 1111 #uuid "9e1fa2ae-92a9-541e-85e9-566450abe17c" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354513 1034 "3.25" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535543 true] [17592186354513 1111 #uuid "dbe9e337-0273-5427-8669-36ffc1e38b0f" 13194139535543 true] [17592186354514 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354515 13194139535543 true] [17592186354514 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354516 1319413953