e distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =1.30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+3\\cdot (1.30)) \\\\  & =1.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.08.\n$$" 131941395355